PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): lay out sparse coalesce's
feature tiles by the launched block width.

WHY
Tensor.coalesce() on a sparse COO tensor whose dense part is wider than 160
elements leaves the tail of every 256-wide tile of each output row
uninitialised (the output is at::empty). Reproduce: indices with duplicates,
values of shape [4, 256]; compare
torch.sparse_coo_tensor(idx, v, (n, 256)).coalesce().values() against the CPU.
With a dense width of 256, 96 features are never written; with 512, 192; with
1024, 384. Widths up to 160 are unaffected, which is why a check with widths 64
and 128 passes.

SparseCUDATensor.cu launches coalesceValuesKernel with
block(at::cuda::warp_size(), 4) and grid.y = ceil(stride / (warp_size * 4)),
which is 64 on gfx803. The kernel walks four features per thread at
startFeature + ii * C10_WARP_SIZE, and c10/macros/Macros.h makes
C10_WARP_SIZE 32 in device code for every AMD target except gfx9. Each tile
of 64 * 4 = 256 features is therefore visited as x + 32 * ii for x in 0..63,
ii in 0..3: features 0..159, with 32..127 visited twice (the duplicate writes
store the same value) and 160..255 never. Reach: nn.Embedding(sparse=True)
gradients and SparseAdam with an embedding dimension above 160.

WHAT
The inner stride is blockDim.x, the width the launcher already used to size
the grid, in both coalesceValuesKernel variants (the numeric one and the bool
one). Every feature of a tile is visited exactly once and nothing depends on
C10_WARP_SIZE. Where the block width already equals C10_WARP_SIZE (CUDA, gfx9)
the behaviour is unchanged.

VERIFICATION (RX 590, gfx803, image built with this patch): tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/sparse_coalesce_check.py
compares coalesce() of COO tensors with duplicate indices against a float64 CPU result for dense widths from 1 to 1024
around the 160 and 256 boundaries, in fp32, fp16 and bool. Without the patch 23 cases fail (features 160 and up of each
256-wide tile are wrong); with it none do.

